  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Organized east/west orientated line of storms \nlooks to develop along and south of the 460 corridor in \nVirginia, leading to the possibility for flash flooding, strong \ndamaging wind gusts, and an isolated tornado through the \nafternoon and evening hours.\n\nThe frontal boundary that produced showers and thunderstorms\nacross portions of the area on Tuesday is expected to lay out in\nan east/west orientation in the vicinity of the 460 corridor\ntoday. Recent CAM guidance appears to be in agreement of this\norganized line of training storms between the 460 corridor and\nthe VA/NC border. Given this strong model agreement in CAM\nguidance, the flood watch will be continued through the NRV and\nsurrounding counties and east into the Piedmonts of NC/VA\nthrough Wednesday evening for the potential for flash flooding\nin this zone. On the east side of this boundary where the\nenvironment will have more instability to work with, and winds\nat the surface look to remain backed, there is the potential for\nstrong damaging wind gusts and a tornado or two. While the\nenvironment does not look as favorable for severe weather and\ntornadoes compared to Tuesday, there is still a risk as deep\nlayer shear in the 0-6km column remains elevated at around \n30-40 knots, 0-3 km SRH values look to remain in the 100 m2/s2\nthreshold, and SCAPE values look to remain in the 1500-2000 J/Kg\nrange. These values, while not as impressive as Tuesdays are\nstill elevated enough to cause concern across Piedmont\nlocations. \n\nOverall, the largest concern with the aforementioned surface\nboundary will be flash flooding as storms look to train in an\neast/west orientation through the early morning and afternoon\ntimeframe. PWATS in the 1.8-2.2 inch range will make for\nextremely efficient rainfall rates across the area, making 4-6\ninch per hour rates very common. With rates this high, and\nstorms training, there is a high likelihood that flash flooding\nwill occur across the aforementioned zone today. It is not out \nof the question that a stripe or zone of 2 to locally 5 inches \nwith higher amounts possible to occur along/north of the VA/NC \nborder counties today with repeated storms over the same \nlocations. Not until after sunset do storms start to finally \nlook to push east into the coastal plain of Virginia and North \nCarolina.\n\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Temperatures should turn more seasonable by the\nmiddle of the week after a frontal passage.\n\nAfter the front finally pushes completely through the area\ntonight, the area looks to dry out and cool down for a couple of\ndays to end the work week. Behind the passage of the front, \nupper level troughing looks to settle across the eastern CONUS \nthrough much of the work week. This will keep 500mb heights much\nlower across the region leading to overall cooler temperatures.\nSurface high pressure coming out of Canada will also lower \ndewpoints into the 50s and 60s which will make it feel much more\ncomfortable outside during the peak heat of the day. Highs look\nto only climb into the low to mid 70s across the mountains, and\nmid to upper 70s across the Piedmont starting Thursday, and \ncontinuing through Saturday before temperatures look to increase\nagain on Sunday of next week. Shower and thunderstorm chances \nbeyond the frontal passage Tuesday night will primarily be \nrelegated to mountain locations, and look to remain isolated, \nwhere orographical lift can enhance development during peak \nheating. While chances look to be minimal on Thursday, they \nslowly start to increase on Friday and into the weekend.",
